ms of what we'll  do later however there is one fact about  the equation that is so vital we will  use it again and again in future videos  that one fact is so important that it's  all we need to understand quantum  computers  to prove the no-cloning theorem and  quantum teleportation and discuss the  many-worlds interpretation of quantum  mechanics plus basically everything else  we're going to do so here it is time  evolution is linear let me explain that  suppose I have an object and there are  two different states I can start the  object in for example I could just drop  this object so it has no velocity to  begin or I could throw it so it has a  big starting velocity we know what would  happen to the object in each of those  cases right we apply a time revolution  to each and this is how they evolve but  now suppose my object didn't start in  one of these two states but actually a  superposition of both in this case it  both had no velocity and a big velocity  to start so what happens to it over time  what do you expect happens think about  it  hopefully what you expect is that it  will be a superposition of the evolution  of each of the states so at each point  in time the state looks like this the  mathematical way to put this is the time  evolution of a superposition is the  linear combination of the time evolution  of each of the states this is what I  mean if you have a state in a  superposition like this to get the  overall future state you just look at  the future state of the individual parts  and then add them back up it even holds  if you write up that same state in a  different basis for example if I change  to the momentum basis here the same rule  applies and so then these two states are  equal to each other they're both the  future state so the general statement of  linearity is this and this does seem  like a reasonable thing to request from  quantum mechanics but linearity has some  subtle philosophical statements about  superposition hidden in 